{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"30886017da738717","type":"text","text":"https://roadmap.sh/typescript","x":0,"y":0,"width":300,"height":86},
		{"id":"5d901cab6b54f751","type":"text","text":"## TypeScript (compiled) vs JavaScript (interpreted)\n\nTypeScript and JavaScript are both programming languages used for web development.\n- JavaScript is run directly by the browser (or Node.js) and is dynamically typed.\n- TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript that adds optional static typing. It must be compiled (transpiled) to JavaScript before it runs; the types are checked at compile time and erased from the output.","x":0,"y":511,"width":560,"height":516},
		{"id":"227ef1779248e046","type":"text","text":"TypeScript","x":0,"y":146,"width":140,"height":86,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"46d32aa32bacd66b","type":"text","text":"## TypeScript Types\n\n| Category        | Types                                          |\n| --------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| Primitive Types | boolean, number, string, void, undefined, null |\n| Object Types    | Interface, Class, Enum, Array, Tuple, Object   |\n| Top Types       | unknown, any                                   |\n| Bottom Types    | never                                          |","x":404,"y":0,"width":440,"height":361,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"15c6fd2c88174a59","type":"text","text":"## undefined (uninitialized) vs null (absent)\n\nJavaScript has two primitive values that signal an absent or uninitialized value: `null` (intentionally absent) and `undefined` (never assigned).\n\nTypeScript has two corresponding _types_ with the same names. How they behave depends on the `strictNullChecks` option.\n\nWith `strictNullChecks` **off**, values that might be `null` or `undefined` can still be accessed normally, and `null`/`undefined` can be assigned to a property of any type (like Java or C#). This is a major source of bugs, so TypeScript recommends turning `strictNullChecks` on.\n\nWith `strictNullChecks` **on**, you must test for `null`/`undefined` before using methods or properties of the value, using narrowing (Python analogue: `Optional[str]` + `if x is not None`):\n```ts\nfunction greet(name: string | null) {\n  if (name !== null) {\n    console.log(name.toUpperCase()); // name: string\n  }\n}\n```","x":1140,"y":511,"width":600,"height":824},
		{"id":"ef0dd21ce2c4bb91","type":"text","text":"## Void Type\n\nThe `void` type in TypeScript represents the absence of a value. It is commonly used as the return type of functions that do not return any value.","x":660,"y":511,"width":380,"height":266},
		{"id":"1f118a20359debaf","type":"text","text":"## Interface and Classes\n\nRoughly:\n\n- TypeScript `interface` corresponds to **`typing.Protocol`**, **abstract type hints**, or sometimes just a documented object shape in Python.\n- TypeScript `class` corresponds to a normal **Python class**.","x":1840,"y":511,"width":400,"height":460},
		{"id":"c6dfd2ed5928cbc3","type":"text","text":"**Type Inference**","x":505,"y":1826,"width":210,"height":86,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"6cfbc91845bd1d02","type":"text","text":"**Type Compatibility**","x":485,"y":1972,"width":250,"height":86,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"5d395cbd80122f9b","type":"text","text":"**Combining Types**","x":500,"y":2118,"width":220,"height":86,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"057cf4a255f5ecb9","type":"text","text":"**Type Guards / Narrowing**","x":460,"y":2264,"width":300,"height":86,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"aaa932193b6686ad","type":"text","text":"## Functions (vs Python): basics\n\n### 1. Simple add function\n\n```python\ndef add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    return a + b\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction add(a: number, b: number): number {\n  return a + b;\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Function with optional parameter\n\n```python\ndef greet(name: str, title: str | None = None) -> str:\n    if title:\n        return f\"Hello, {title} {name}\"\n    return f\"Hello, {name}\"\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction greet(name: string, title?: string): string {\n  if (title) {\n    return `Hello, ${title} ${name}`;\n  }\n  return `Hello, ${name}`;\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Arrow function / lambda-style equivalent\n\n```python\nsquare = lambda x: x * x\n```\n\n```ts\nconst square = (x: number): number => x * x;\n```","x":860,"y":1926,"width":640,"height":1140,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"3c7c465b0fde85fa","type":"text","text":"## Class\n```python\nclass User:\n    def __init__(self, id: int, name: str):\n        self.id = id\n        self.name = name\n\n    def greet(self) -> str:\n        return f\"Hello, {self.name}\"\n\nu = User(1, \"Ju\")\n```\n```ts\nclass User {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n\n  constructor(id: number, name: string) {\n    this.id = id;\n    this.name = name;\n  }\n\n  greet(): string {\n    return `Hello, ${this.name}`;\n  }\n}\n\nconst u = new User(1, \"Ju\");\n```\nShorthand: `constructor(public id: number, public name: string) {}` declares and assigns the fields in one go (parameter properties).","x":2380,"y":511,"width":540,"height":867},
		{"id":"432173db7ce3387c","type":"text","text":"## Access Modifiers\nAccess modifiers control the visibility of class members (properties and methods):\n- `public` (default): accessible everywhere.\n- `protected`: accessible in the class and its subclasses.\n- `private`: accessible only inside the class.\n\nPython has no enforced equivalent; `_name` and `__name` are conventions. TypeScript checks these only at compile time; for true runtime privacy use JavaScript `#name` fields.","x":3020,"y":511,"width":400,"height":688},
		{"id":"08c82b5692fdc865","type":"text","text":"**TypeScript Interfaces**","x":3820,"y":1826,"width":280,"height":86,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"8e9e6ad7ca2b7ae2","type":"text","text":"## Interface\n```python\nfrom typing import Protocol\n\nclass UserLike(Protocol):\n    id: int\n    name: str\n```\n```ts\ninterface UserLike {\n  id: number;\n  name: string;\n}\n\nconst u: UserLike = { id: 1, name: \"Ju\" };\n```\nBoth are structural: any object with the right shape matches, no explicit inheritance needed.","x":1840,"y":1031,"width":440,"height":575},
		{"id":"34e71b8d200cec08","type":"text","text":"**Classes**","x":4200,"y":1826,"width":140,"height":86,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"2766ca2306332193","type":"text","text":"**Generics**","x":4195,"y":1972,"width":150,"height":86,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"ad4865e25858592e","type":"text","text":"## Utility and Advanced Types\nUtility Types in TypeScript are built-in generic types that perform common type transformations.","x":4455,"y":1926,"width":340,"height":273,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"fab865f21808e91b","type":"text","text":"| TypeScript Utility / Feature | What it does in TypeScript | Closest Python equivalent | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `Partial<T>` | Makes all properties in `T` optional | `TypedDict(..., total=False)` | Best match for dict-like typed objects |\n| `Pick<T, K>` | Selects a subset of keys from `T` | New `TypedDict` / `dataclass` with only those fields | Usually manual |\n| `Omit<T, K>` | Removes keys `K` from `T` | New `TypedDict` / `dataclass` without those fields | Usually manual |\n| `Readonly<T>` | Makes properties read-only | `@dataclass(frozen=True)` / `NamedTuple` | Closest immutability match |\n| `Record<K, V>` | Object type with keys `K` and values `V` | `dict[K, V]` | Direct analogue |\n| `Exclude<T, U>` | Removes from union `T` anything assignable to `U` | No direct built-in | Usually modeled by writing a narrower `Union` |\n| `Extract<T, U>` | Keeps from union `T` only members assignable to `U` | No direct built-in | Usually modeled manually |\n| `Awaited<T>` | Unwraps the resolved type of a `Promise` | Result type of `Awaitable[T]` / `Coroutine[Any, Any, T]` | No built-in unwrapping utility |\n| `Parameters<T>` | Tuple of a function’s parameter types | `ParamSpec` | Best typing-level analogue |\n| `NonNullable<T>` | Removes `null` and `undefined` from `T` | Remove `None` from `Optional[T]` / use plain `T` | Conceptual only |\n| `ReturnType<T>` | Gets a function’s return type | `Callable[..., R]` | `R` is the return type |\n| `InstanceType<T>` | Gets the instance type produced by a constructor | The class instance type itself | Python classes are already first-class types |\n| Mapped Types | Transform each property of a type | `TypedDict` transformations / metaprogramming / decorators | No direct built-in generic type transform |\n| Conditional Types | Choose one type or another based on a type relationship | Overloads, `TypeGuard`, `typing_extensions.TypeIs`, manual `TypeVar` bounds | Python typing is less expressive here |\n| Literal Types | Restrict a value to specific constants | `Literal[...]` | Direct analogue via `typing.Literal` |\n| Template Literal Types | Build string types from string patterns | `Literal[...]` for fixed strings only | No true pattern-composition equivalent in Python typing |\n| Recursive Types | Define a type in terms of itself | Recursive aliases, forward refs, `from __future__ import annotations` | Very close analogue |","x":4895,"y":1926,"width":1150,"height":1128},
		{"id":"68ead00cc23f9d94","type":"text","text":"**Decorators**","x":4185,"y":2118,"width":170,"height":86,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"ce9e22840712696a","type":"text","text":"## TypeScript Modules\n\nModules in TypeScript (and JavaScript) are a way to organize code into reusable and manageable blocks. ","x":4455,"y":3503,"width":340,"height":265,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"684575ac86c11514","type":"text","text":"**TypeScript Ecosystem**","x":4895,"y":3592,"width":270,"height":86,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"749f4e9d12a804da","type":"text","text":"## External Modules in TypeScript\n\nExternal modules in TypeScript are files containing code that can be imported and used in other files. They help organize and structure your project by dividing code into logical units, improving maintainability and reusability. Each external module has its own scope, preventing naming conflicts and allowing you to explicitly control what is exposed from the module using `export` and what is consumed from other modules using `import`.","x":2820,"y":3918,"width":380,"height":480},
		{"id":"bfabd8fbdecc88f7","type":"text","text":"## Namespaces\n\nNamespaces in TypeScript are a way to organize code into logical groups, preventing naming collisions and improving code maintainability. They create a named container where you can define variables, functions, classes, and interfaces. By using namespaces, you can encapsulate related functionality under a specific name, making your code more structured and easier to understand.","x":4400,"y":3918,"width":380,"height":418},
		{"id":"b7bc67a063686762","type":"text","text":"## Namespace Augmentation\n\nNamespace augmentation in TypeScript allows you to add new properties or methods to an existing namespace, even if that namespace is defined in a separate file or module. This feature is particularly useful for extending namespaces declared in external libraries or modules without directly modifying the original source code. It provides a way to customize or add functionality to existing namespaces in a modular and organized manner.","x":1580,"y":3918,"width":380,"height":470},
		{"id":"8e6d04efa2e913c5","type":"text","text":"## Global Augmentation\n\nGlobal augmentation in TypeScript lets you add or modify existing global types and interfaces. This is particularly useful when working with JavaScript libraries that don't have TypeScript definitions or when you want to extend existing TypeScript definitions to suit your specific project needs. It essentially allows you to declare properties or methods on globally available objects (like `window` or built-in JavaScript objects) from within your TypeScript code.","x":0,"y":3918,"width":380,"height":496},
		{"id":"11e35dc41898b8af","type":"text","text":"What each part means:\n\n- `declare global { ... }` says: “the declarations inside here are added to the global scope, not just this module.”\n- `declare` says: “this is only a type declaration; it does not generate JavaScript.”\n- `declare global` is only allowed inside a **module**, i.e. a file with at least one `import` or `export`. `export {};` is the minimal way to make a file a module.\n```ts\nexport {};\n\ndeclare global {\n  interface Window {\n    myFeatureFlag: boolean;\n  }\n}\n\nwindow.myFeatureFlag; // now type-checks\n```\nNote: `export declare global` is **not** valid; `declare global` itself cannot be exported.","x":480,"y":3918,"width":480,"height":814},
		{"id":"a62b8d3ad03bfa42","type":"text","text":"Why `export {};`?\n\nIt does not export any actual value. It is just a marker that tells TypeScript:\n\n> “Treat this file as a module (own scope), not as a global script.”\n\nIf the file already has a real `import` or `export`, it is not needed.","x":1060,"y":3918,"width":420,"height":469},
		{"id":"69ff0c9121ec8ec6","type":"text","text":"### 1) Original library/module\n```ts\n// math-utils.ts\nexport namespace MathUtils {\n  export function add(a: number, b: number): number {\n    return a + b;\n  }\n}\n```\n### 2) Augment the namespace elsewhere\n```ts\n// math-utils.extra.ts\nimport { MathUtils } from \"./math-utils\";\n\n// namespace augmentation: types only\ndeclare module \"./math-utils\" {\n  export namespace MathUtils {\n    export let multiply: (a: number, b: number) => number;\n  }\n}\n\n// attach the implementation at runtime\nMathUtils.multiply = (a, b) => a * b;\n```\nDeclared as `let` (not `function`), because TypeScript does not allow assigning to a function declaration.\n\n### 3) Use it\n```ts\n// app.ts\nimport { MathUtils } from \"./math-utils\";\nimport \"./math-utils.extra\";\n\nconsole.log(MathUtils.add(2, 3));      // 5\nconsole.log(MathUtils.multiply(2, 3)); // 6\n```","x":2060,"y":3918,"width":660,"height":1079},
		{"id":"42fc132ead3d31f2","type":"text","text":"## Import external modules\n\n### `user.ts`\n```ts\n// user.ts\nexport interface User {  \n  id: number;  \n  name: string;  \n}  \n  \nexport function getUser(id: number): User {  \n  return {  \n    id,  \n    name: \"Alice\",  \n  };  \n}\n```\n### `index.ts`\n```ts\n// index.ts\nimport { getUser, type User } from \"./user\";  \n  \nconst user: User = getUser(1);  \nconsole.log(user.name);\n```","x":3300,"y":3918,"width":500,"height":776},
		{"id":"0e7af9a74b0e7802","type":"text","text":"## Ambient Modules\n\nAmbient modules (`declare module \"name\" { ... }`, usually placed in a `.d.ts` declaration file) describe the shape of existing JavaScript code. They tell the compiler that a module exists and what it exports, so you can import a library not written in TypeScript without type errors. Similar to Python `.pyi` stub files.\n```ts\n// types/legacy-lib.d.ts\ndeclare module \"legacy-lib\" {\n  export function parse(s: string): number;\n}\n```","x":3900,"y":3918,"width":400,"height":535},
		{"id":"4d7b26d5f88ed31f","type":"text","text":"## Functions (vs Python): defaults, arrays, objects\n\n### 4. Function with default parameter\n\n```python\ndef multiply(a: int, b: int = 2) -> int:\n    return a * b\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction multiply(a: number, b: number = 2): number {\n  return a * b;\n}\n```\n\n### 5. Function returning a list / array\n\n```python\ndef get_even_numbers(numbers: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    return [n for n in numbers if n % 2 == 0]\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction getEvenNumbers(numbers: number[]): number[] {\n  return numbers.filter((n) => n % 2 === 0);\n}\n```\n\n### 6. Function with object parameter\n\n```python\nfrom typing import TypedDict\n\nclass User(TypedDict):\n    name: str\n    age: int\n\ndef format_user(user: User) -> str:\n    return f\"{user['name']} is {user['age']} years old\"\n```\n\n```ts\ntype User = {\n  name: string;\n  age: number;\n};\n\nfunction formatUser(user: User): string {\n  return `${user.name} is ${user.age} years old`;\n}\n```","x":1600,"y":1926,"width":640,"height":1357,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"cb6ab970fa2f993e","type":"text","text":"## Functions (vs Python): async, callbacks, rest\n\n### 7. Async function\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def fetch_data() -> str:\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)\n    return \"data loaded\"\n```\n\n```ts\nasync function fetchData(): Promise<string> {\n  return new Promise((resolve) => {\n    setTimeout(() => resolve(\"data loaded\"), 1000);\n  });\n}\n```\n\n### 8. Callback function\n\n```python\nfrom collections.abc import Callable\n\ndef process(value: int, callback: Callable[[int], int]) -> int:\n    return callback(value)\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction process(value: number, callback: (x: number) => number): number {\n  return callback(value);\n}\n```\n\n### 9. Rest parameters / variadic arguments\n\n```python\ndef sum_all(*numbers: int) -> int:\n    return sum(numbers)\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction sumAll(...numbers: number[]): number {\n  return numbers.reduce((total, n) => total + n, 0);\n}\n```","x":2340,"y":1926,"width":640,"height":1257,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"55f33079284aebba","type":"text","text":"## Functions (vs Python): generics, unions, higher-order\n\n### 10. Generic function\n\n```python\nfrom typing import TypeVar\n\nT = TypeVar(\"T\")\n\ndef first_item(items: list[T]) -> T:\n    return items[0]\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction firstItem<T>(items: T[]): T {\n  return items[0];\n}\n```\n\n### 11. Function with union-like behavior\n\n```python\ndef stringify(value: int | str) -> str:\n    return str(value)\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction stringify(value: number | string): string {\n  return String(value);\n}\n```\n\n### 12. Higher-order function\n\n```python\nfrom collections.abc import Callable\n\ndef make_multiplier(factor: int) -> Callable[[int], int]:\n    def multiplier(x: int) -> int:\n        return x * factor\n    return multiplier\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction makeMultiplier(factor: number): (x: number) => number {\n  return (x: number) => x * factor;\n}\n```","x":3080,"y":1926,"width":640,"height":1296,"color":"1"}
	],
